Below is a minimal, self‑contained example that shows how you can **serialize a `RootObjectMapper` to XContent** while:

* **Including all default settings** (so that the output contains the default values for dynamic date formats, dynamic templates, date detection, numeric detection, etc.).
* **Skipping runtime fields** (so that any runtime fields that might exist on the mapper are omitted from the output).

The example uses the `RootObjectMapper_doXContent` helper that you mentioned.  
Because the helper is a *method* on the `RootObjectMapper` instance, you only need to supply the two arguments (`builder` and `params`).  
Everything else – the mapper itself and its configuration – is handled by the instance you call the method on.

---

## 1. Build the `RootObjectMapper` with the desired defaults

```java
// 1️⃣  Create a RootObjectMapper with the default settings you want.
//     (The actual builder API may vary – adjust the names if your version differs.)

RootObjectMapper rootObjectMapper = new RootObjectMapper.Builder("my_index")   // <-- index name
        // 2️⃣  Enable date detection (default is true, but we set it explicitly for clarity)
        .dateDetection(true)

        // 3️⃣  Enable numeric detection (default is true)
        .numericDetection(true)

        // 4️⃣  Add a default dynamic template (example: match all fields and treat them as keyword)
        .dynamicTemplates(Arrays.asList(
                new DynamicTemplate(
                        "match_all_as_keyword",
                        new MatchMappingType("match", "*"),
                        new MappingType("keyword")
                )
        ))

        // 5️⃣  Set the default dynamic date format (the standard one is usually enough)
        .dynamicDateFormats(Arrays.asList("strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis"))

        // 6️⃣  Build the mapper
        .build();
```

> **Note**  
> *If you’re using a different version of Elasticsearch, the exact builder methods may differ.  
> The important part is that the mapper is configured with the defaults you care about.*

---

## 2. Prepare the `XContentBuilder` instance

```java
// 7️⃣  Create an XContentBuilder that will receive the serialized mapper.
//     Here we use JSON format and enable pretty printing for readability.

XContentBuilder builder = XContentFactory.jsonBuilder()
        .prettyPrint();   // <-- optional, just makes the output easier to read
```

---

## 3. Prepare the parameters array

```java
// 8️⃣  Build the ArrayList of parameters that the helper expects.
//     The order matters: first element → includeDefaults, second → skipRuntimeFields.

ArrayList<Object> params = new ArrayList<>();
params.add(Boolean.TRUE);   // includeDefaults = true
params.add(Boolean.TRUE);   // skipRuntimeFields = true
```

---

## 4. Call the helper

```java
// 9️⃣  Serialize the mapper to XContent.
//     The helper is a method on the mapper instance, so we simply invoke it.

rootObjectMapper.doXContent(builder, params);
```

---

## 5. Inspect the result

```java
// 10️⃣  Convert the builder to a string (or write it to a file, etc.)
String jsonOutput = builder.string();

System.out.println(jsonOutput);
```

### Expected output (simplified)

```json
{
  "_meta": {
    "dynamic_templates": [
      {
        "match_all_as_keyword": {
          "match_mapping_type": "match",
          "match": "*",
          "mapping": {
            "type": "keyword"
          }
        }
      }
    ],
    "dynamic_date_formats": [
      "strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis"
    ],
    "date_detection": true,
    "numeric_detection": true
  },
  "properties": {
    // ... any properties defined on the mapper ...
  }
}
```

> **Key points**  
> * The `includeDefaults=true` flag ensures that the output contains the default values for all settings, even if they were not explicitly set on the mapper.  
> * The `skipRuntimeFields=true` flag tells the serializer to omit any runtime fields that might be present.  
> * All other settings (dynamic date formats, dynamic templates, date detection, numeric detection) are included because they are part of the mapper’s configuration.

---

## Full Java snippet

```java
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentBuilder;
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentFactory;
import org.elasticsearch.index.mapper.RootObjectMapper;
import org.elasticsearch.index.mapper.DynamicTemplate;
import org.elasticsearch.index.mapper.MappingType;
import org.elasticsearch.index.mapper.MatchMappingType;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;

public class RootObjectMapperExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        // 1️⃣ Build the mapper
        RootObjectMapper rootObjectMapper = new RootObjectMapper.Builder("my_index")
                .dateDetection(true)
                .numericDetection(true)
                .dynamicTemplates(Arrays.asList(
                        new DynamicTemplate(
                                "match_all_as_keyword",
                                new MatchMappingType("match", "*"),
                                new MappingType("keyword")
                        )
                ))
                .dynamicDateFormats(Arrays.asList("strict_date_optional_time||epoch_millis"))
                .build();

        // 2️⃣ Create the builder
        XContentBuilder builder = XContentFactory.jsonBuilder()
                .prettyPrint();

        // 3️⃣ Prepare params
        ArrayList<Object> params = new ArrayList<>();
        params.add(Boolean.TRUE);   // includeDefaults
        params.add(Boolean.TRUE);   // skipRuntimeFields

        // 4️⃣ Serialize
        rootObjectMapper.doXContent(builder, params);

        // 5️⃣ Print
        System.out.println(builder.string());
    }
}
```

Replace the placeholder classes (`DynamicTemplate`, `MappingType`, `MatchMappingType`) with the actual ones from your Elasticsearch version if they differ. The core idea remains the same: **create the mapper, build the builder, set the parameters, and call `doXContent`.**
